//! ui/effect — the effects a user interface platform grants.
//!
//! The same shape the standard library's other effect declarations have: an
//! effect is an interface declared with `effect` instead of `trait`, only a
//! platform module may declare one, and its implementors are effect-carrying,
//! so they may be passed only as `self` or `ctx` (SPEC 10.1, 10.2).
//!
//! Everything here rests on one idea: **a signal handle is inert data, and the
//! authority to read or write it travels through `ctx`.** That is the split
//! `Alloc` and `Region` already use — the handle is the reference, the context
//! is the effect. `Signal<T>` and `Prop<T>`, in `ui/signal` and `ui/prop`, are
//! ordinary capturable data; nothing in them can do anything without one of
//! the effects below.
//!
//! A signal identifier is an `Int` here rather than a `Signal<T>` because this
//! module is the boundary with the runtime, which holds one graph of untyped
//! cells. The typed handle is built on top, in ordinary Buri.
//!
//! **Requests are not here.** There used to be a third effect, `Fetch`, whose
//! whole reason was that `Net.fetch` blocked and a blocked page is a frozen
//! one — so it took a callback and answered later. A suspending call no longer
//! blocks anything, so a page asks for `Net` like every other platform and
//! writes the answer on the line after the question. The callback shape, the
//! second `Request`, the second error enum and the `Aborted` that only it had
//! are all gone; `NetError.Aborted` is where that last one lives now.

/// Reading signals, alone.
///
/// Separate from `Ui` so a computation can be handed read authority and
/// provably nothing else: a `fn(Scope) => T` can observe the graph and cannot
/// change it, which is what makes a derivation safe to re-run whenever the
/// runtime likes.
export effect Watch {
  /// The current value of the cell `id` names.
  ///
  /// Reading inside a computation records a dependency, so the computation
  /// re-runs when that cell changes. Reading outside one records nothing.
  fn read<T>(self, id: Int): T;
}

/// Creating and writing signals, and registering computations.
///
/// `read` appears here as well as on `Watch` because writing is not a
/// substitute for reading: `Signal.update` reads the old value and writes a
/// new one, and it takes the effect that can write. An implementor of `Ui`
/// grants strictly more than one of `Watch`.
export effect Ui {
  /// A fresh cell holding `initial`. The returned `Int` names it for the
  /// lifetime of the program.
  fn signal<T>(self, initial: T): Int;
  /// The current value of the cell `id` names.
  fn read<T>(self, id: Int): T;
  /// Replaces the value of the cell `id` names, marking everything that read
  /// it out of date.
  fn write<T>(self, id: Int, value: T): ();
  /// A derived cell: `compute` runs on demand and is re-run only after
  /// something it read has changed. Lazy, so a memo nothing reads never runs.
  fn memo<T>(self, compute: fn(Scope) => T): Int;
  /// A computation run for its effect on the world rather than its value. It
  /// runs once immediately — which is how it learns what it depends on — and
  /// again whenever any of that changes.
  ///
  /// Named `watch` rather than `effect` because `effect` is a keyword.
  fn watch(self, run: fn(Scope) => ()): ();
}

/// The one concrete implementor of `Watch`, minted only by the runtime when it
/// evaluates a reactive closure.
///
/// Concrete so that closure types can name it — which is what keeps `Prop`
/// free of a *context* type parameter, and will keep `Style` free of one. Its
/// field is private, so no program can build one; it implements an effect, so
/// it is effect-carrying and may only arrive as `ctx`. A signature taking a
/// `Scope` is visibly effectful.
export struct Scope(Int);

impl Watch for Scope {
  fn read<T>(self, id: Int): T;
}

/// Something a reader did.
///
/// Opaque in this version, and deliberately: it exists so that a handler that
/// later wants to know *where* a press landed, or whether a modifier key was
/// down, gains an accessor rather than a new signature. There are two shapes
/// of handler in the vocabulary — a press, on a button, and a submission, on a
/// form — and both are handed one of these.
///
/// A change is not among them, and that is not an omission: a field and a
/// toggle are bound to a `Signal`, so what the reader typed is in the signal
/// already. Hover, focus and pressed are not among them either, because they
/// are appearance rather than events, and belong to `ui/style` — a hover that
/// wrote a signal would put a render in the path of every mouse move.
///
/// Holds no authority, so a lambda may capture one. Its field is private, so
/// only the runtime can mint one.
export struct Event(Int);
